        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        A handful of topical ingredients have real clinical evidence behind them for reducing the look of dark spots and uneven skin tone, but most products pair them poorly, skip sun protection, or set timelines no serum can meet.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Niacinamide and kojic acid have the strongest human evidence for helping skin look more even and reducing the appearance of dark spots with daily topical use. Results take a minimum of four to eight weeks, and they stop working the moment daily sunscreen stops, because UV exposure continuously creates new pigment. Patch test any new serum before full use, and see a dermatologist for pigmentation that is painful, rapidly spreading, or connected to a medical condition.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If you have looked at the skincare aisle recently, every second product promises to fade dark spots. The reality is more selective. A few ingredients have been tested on real people, measured with instruments, and shown to reduce the look of pigmentation over weeks of consistent use. Most ingredients on labels have not. This article covers what the research shows, what realistic timelines look like, and how to build a routine that does not work against itself.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Why dark spots form in the first place
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Skin colour comes from melanin, a pigment produced by specialist cells called melanocytes. Those cells respond to signals: UV light, inflammation from a pimple, friction, and hormonal shifts can all trigger more pigment production than the skin would normally make. The result is a darker patch where melanin has concentrated. Post-acne marks, sun spots, and perioral pigmentation (darker skin around the mouth, common on Indian skin) all form this way.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The patch fades because skin renews itself. Cells from the deeper layers move upward and shed, carrying pigment with them. But if the trigger keeps firing (more sun, another breakout, no SPF), new pigment is being added faster than the old pigment leaves. That is why the most important thing in any pigmentation routine is the one most people treat as optional: sunscreen, every day.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        The ingredients with the clearest evidence
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Not every brightening ingredient has been tested in a controlled human trial. The ones below have, and the evidence varies, so it helps to know what was actually measured and in whom.
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Niacinamide.</strong> A 2002 clinical study found that 5% niacinamide significantly reduced hyperpigmentation and increased skin lightness in subjects who applied it twice daily for four weeks, compared to a vehicle control.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12100180/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;">1</a></sup> The effect appears to work by interrupting the transfer of pigment packets from melanocytes to the surface skin cells. A 2022 systematic review of topical treatments for post-inflammatory pigmentation included niacinamide among the agents with high-quality supporting studies.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34525885/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;">2</a></sup> It is also well tolerated and tends not to irritate.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Kojic acid.</strong> Kojic acid blocks the enzyme that starts melanin production. In a split-face clinical study, a formulation containing kojic acid alongside glycolic acid produced better results than the combination without it: more than 50% clearance was seen in 60% of patients on the kojic acid side, compared with 47.5% on the other.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10417583/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;">3</a></sup> It is commonly paired with niacinamide because the two act at different points in the pigment pathway.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Vitamin C.</strong> Topical vitamin C supports a more even-looking tone. It is fragile and can oxidise before it works, so serum packaging and formula chemistry matter more here than with most other ingredients. An opaque or airtight bottle is a good sign.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Bio-retinoids (plant-based retinol alternatives).</strong> Bio-retinoids drive surface renewal without the sun sensitivity or early peeling that retinol beginners often experience. They support texture and visible pore appearance and can be used morning and night, though they work more slowly than prescription retinoids.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What to expect, and when
    </h2>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Two weeks.</strong> Skin usually feels more comfortable if the formula has ceramides or barrier ingredients. Visible tone change is unlikely this early.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Four to six weeks.</strong> This is when most people first notice a shift in an individual spot, provided they have been consistent every day and have used sunscreen every morning.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Eight to twelve weeks.</strong> Significant reduction in the look of dark spots and overall tone evenness is measurable at this point in clinical studies. Stopping sunscreen before here resets much of the progress.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        No topical serum removes pigmentation overnight. The skin renews on its own schedule, and a serum works with that renewal. Someone using a brightening serum in summer without SPF will likely see no progress, because UV creating new pigment outpaces what the serum can interrupt.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
            The LNGVTY system
        </p>
        <h2 style="margin:0 0 12px;color:#FFFFFF;font-size:31px;line-height:1.15;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.8px;">
            Choose by skin concern
        </h2>
