    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        How to use a brightening serum: the basics
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The steps below apply whether you are using the <a href="/products/dark-spots-pore-control-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Dark Spots, Pore Control Serum</a> or any other brightening serum. Active ingredients need a clean, damp surface and a clear path to the skin.
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Cleanse.</strong> Wash your face and pat it mostly dry but leave the skin slightly damp. Actives absorb better on damp skin than on dry.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Apply the serum.</strong> Use 3 to 4 drops across the face. Press or pat the serum in rather than rubbing, especially over areas of pigmentation. One serum is enough at the active step; do not layer several actives over each other.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Moisturise.</strong> A moisturiser seals the active step and keeps the skin barrier comfortable. A happy barrier means less inflammation, which means fewer new pigmentation triggers.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Sunscreen in the morning (every morning).</strong> This is not optional. Skipping SPF while using brightening actives means UV is continuously adding new pigment as the serum is trying to reduce old pigment. SPF 30 or above, broad-spectrum.</li>
    </ol>

    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            Patch test any new serum before applying it all over your face. Apply a small amount to the inside of your wrist or behind your ear, wait 24 hours, and check for redness or irritation. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding, check with your doctor before using kojic acid or any new active ingredient.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Comparing the most common serum ingredients for pigmentation
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The table below compares the most widely studied options so you can read a label with some context.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:24px 0 34px;overflow-x:auto;border-top:2px solid #181818;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);">
        <table style="width:100%;min-width:620px;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;">
            <thead>
                <tr style="background:#FFFEF3;">
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Ingredient</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Best suited to</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">What to know</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Niacinamide</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Post-inflammatory marks, general uneven tone, sensitive skin</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Well tolerated, can be used morning and night, good barrier and pore benefits alongside tone</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Kojic acid</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Sun spots, stubborn dark patches, melasma (under dermatologist guidance)</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Works at the melanin production step; some people experience mild irritation; often paired with niacinamide</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Vitamin C</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Overall dullness, mild uneven tone, antioxidant protection</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Formula stability matters; oxidised vitamin C (yellow-brown serum) has lost its activity</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;font-weight:700;">Bio-retinoids</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Texture, visible pores, surface renewal alongside tone work</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Gentler than retinol; can be used morning and night; slower than prescription retinoids</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Pigmentation in Indian skin: what changes
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Darker skin tones produce more melanin and distribute it more actively, which means pigmentation from a breakout, a rash, or even friction tends to last longer and show more visibly. Sun damage often shows as uneven patches rather than a uniform tan, particularly around the mouth and cheeks. This matters for product choice because some ingredients that work well on lighter skin can irritate darker skin, and irritation itself triggers more pigmentation. Niacinamide's good tolerability record makes it especially suitable here. Kojic acid at cosmetic concentrations is generally considered safe, but some people do experience mild irritation, so patch testing is worth doing regardless of skin tone.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Post-inflammatory pigmentation can take three to six months to fade on its own. With daily actives and consistent SPF, that timeline can shorten, but it will not compress to weeks. If a mark has been there for over a year and has not shifted, a dermatologist visit is a better next step than a new serum.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>
    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How long does a brightening serum take to work?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Most people see the first visible shift in an individual dark spot between four and six weeks of daily use. Measurable overall tone improvement tends to appear at eight weeks in clinical studies, but only if sunscreen is used every morning.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use a brightening serum and retinol at the same time?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">It is better not to stack multiple actives, especially as a beginner. A serum that already contains niacinamide, kojic acid, and bio-retinoids covers most of the bases without the irritation risk of adding separate retinol. If you are on a prescribed retinoid, check with your dermatologist before adding anything new.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is niacinamide good for post-acne marks?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes. Niacinamide is well tolerated and does not irritate acne-prone skin or trigger new breakouts. Pair it with daily SPF and, if marks are fresh, address any ongoing breakouts first so new pigment triggers stop.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Do I really need sunscreen with a brightening serum?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes, every day. UV exposure continuously drives melanin production. Without SPF, you are competing against a process that runs faster than any serum can interrupt. Broad-spectrum SPF 30 or above, every morning, is the baseline.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is kojic acid safe for Indian skin?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Kojic acid at cosmetic concentrations is generally considered safe and commonly used for pigmentation on South and Southeast Asian skin. Some people experience mild irritation. Patch test first, and avoid it if you have an open skin condition, active eczema, or significant irritation.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use a brightening serum if I am pregnant?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Speak to your doctor before using kojic acid or any new active during pregnancy or while breastfeeding. Many brightening formulas contain ingredients that have not been studied in this population.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">When should I see a dermatologist instead of trying a serum?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">See a dermatologist if a patch is rapidly growing, irregular in border, raised, or painful. Melasma often needs prescription-strength treatment rather than over-the-counter actives alone. No serum should delay evaluation of a skin change that concerns you.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

